  "evidence": "TEXT_G: Calls for a full national debate and a referendum on Britain’s relationship with Europe; proposes a question choosing between a federal Europe and an association of sovereign nations; urges holding the referendum before or with the next General Election; argues that a single-currency-only referendum is inadequate because it evades sovereignty. TEXT_F: Commits to NHS real-terms increases and more GPs/nurses; supports carers with a national Respite Care Programme; promises regeneration of the worst estates; sets an Education Guarantee with interventions in failing schools and wider access (nursery vouchers, apprenticeships); pledges environmental leadership and higher EU animal welfare standards; emphasizes sovereignty as a partnership of nation states with a referendum on the single currency; also rejects the EU Social Chapter and a national minimum wage.",
  "reasoning": "Because Care and Equality weigh most in the profile, TEXT_F’s concrete pledges on healthcare, carers, deprived estates, education, and environmental/animal welfare speak directly to Care (and some equality concerns), whereas TEXT_G focuses almost solely on constitutional sovereignty. Although TEXT_F contains market-oriented and anti–minimum wage positions, it still better fits the overall profile than TEXT_G, which neglects the electorate’s top Care/Egalitarian priorities.",
